{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "Workflow Deviation Detection and Divergence Notifications",
  "description": "How the extension checks an agent run against user-configured 'expected workflows' entirely locally: parse the workflow config, group interactions into turns, match each turn against ordered step predicates (including safe content regexes), surface deviation markers in the session-detail webview, and proactively toast only NEW divergences. No raw content or result ever leaves the machine. Fifth in the series.",
  "nextTour": "Privacy-Safe Aggregation and Cloud Sync",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "schemas/workflow-config.schema.json",
      "line": 4,
      "title": "The workflow config contract",
      "description": "**Situation.** A team can declare what a *correct* agent workflow looks like — an ordered set of expected steps, each with a matching predicate and an optional max duration.\n\n**Mechanism.** This JSON Schema (`Agent Workflow Configuration`) defines that shape: steps, predicates over span attributes, optional content predicates, and `maxDurationMs` budgets.\n\n**Implication.** Deviation detection is *declarative and local* — users describe intent in settings, and the extension flags runs that stray, without any cloud model.\n\n**Gotcha.** This schema governs only local matching; it is unrelated to the aggregate-batch schema (Tour 6) that crosses the network. Different contract, different purpose."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/config/workflowParsing.ts",
      "line": 58,
      "title": "Settings into typed configs",
      "description": "**Situation.** The raw workflow config comes from VS Code settings as untyped JSON and must be validated before use.\n\n**Mechanism.** `parseWorkflowConfigs(raw, defaultMaxMs)` validates and normalizes the input into typed `WorkflowConfig[]`, applying a default duration budget where one isn't specified.\n\n**Implication.** Everything downstream works with trusted, typed configs, so the matching engine never re-validates user input mid-run.\n\n**Gotcha.** Malformed entries are rejected here at the boundary — a bad predicate in settings yields no workflow rather than a crash deep in the detector."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/deviation/models.ts",
      "line": 23,
      "title": "Kinds of divergence",
      "description": "**Situation.** \"Deviation\" isn't one thing — a run can skip an expected step, do something out of order, run too long, or trigger a forbidden action.\n\n**Mechanism.** The `DeviationType` enum enumerates those categories, and each detected `WorkflowDeviation` (line 190) records its type plus the offending turn.\n\n**Implication.** The webview can render different markers per type, and the notifier can describe *why* a run diverged, not just *that* it did.\n\n**Gotcha.** Types are additive — new categories should extend this enum so the renderer and notifier stay exhaustive rather than defaulting to a generic 'diverged'."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/deviation/models.ts",
      "line": 43,
      "title": "What a step matches",
      "description": "**Situation.** Each expected workflow step needs a rule for \"did this turn satisfy me?\".\n\n**Mechanism.** `StepPredicate` describes that rule over span attributes (operation, tool name, etc.), optionally including a `ContentPredicate` (line 105) that inspects a specific attribute's text with a substring or regex.\n\n**Implication.** Matching can be as coarse as \"a tool was called\" or as precise as \"the commit message matched this pattern\", all declaratively.\n\n**Gotcha.** Content predicates read raw attribute text — this is a *local* raw-content read (like the detail panel), and its results never leave the machine."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/deviation/models.ts",
      "line": 72,
      "title": "The content attribute allow-list",
      "description": "**Situation.** Letting a predicate read *any* attribute would be an open-ended surface; content matching is deliberately constrained.\n\n**Mechanism.** `CONTENT_PREDICATE_ATTRIBUTES` is a fixed allow-list of attribute keys a content predicate may target, with an O(1) membership set alongside it.\n\n**Implication.** A workflow author can only match content on a vetted set of fields, keeping the feature predictable and bounded.\n\n**Gotcha.** A predicate naming an attribute outside this list is rejected at parse time — you can't smuggle in matching against an arbitrary field."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/deviation/turnGrouping.ts",
      "line": 23,
      "title": "Grouping interactions into turns",
      "description": "**Situation.** Raw spans are fine-grained (a chat turn, its tool calls, hooks); workflows reason at the granularity of a *turn*.\n\n**Mechanism.** `groupInteractionsByTurn` buckets the ordered interactions into turn groups, so the detector evaluates one coherent unit of agent activity at a time.\n\n**Implication.** Step predicates match against turns, aligning the analysis with how a human reads the session-detail timeline.\n\n**Gotcha.** Grouping order mirrors execution order; a mis-grouped stream would throw off the ordered step matching, so this is the foundation the detector rests on."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/deviation/deviationDetector.ts",
      "line": 108,
      "title": "The core matching loop",
      "description": "**Situation.** With turns grouped and configs parsed, the detector decides whether a run followed its workflow.\n\n**Mechanism.** `detectForTurns(turns, configs, contentLookup)` walks the expected steps against the actual turns, emitting a `WorkflowDeviation` whenever a step is skipped, mis-ordered, over budget, or forbidden.\n\n**Implication.** This pure function is the heart of the feature and is directly unit-testable — turns in, deviations out, no I/O.\n\n**Gotcha.** It takes a `contentLookup` callback rather than reading content itself, keeping the detector pure and letting the caller control how raw text is fetched."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/deviation/deviationDetector.ts",
      "line": 317,
      "title": "Evaluating one predicate",
      "description": "**Situation.** Each step/turn comparison ultimately reduces to \"does this interaction satisfy this predicate?\".\n\n**Mechanism.** `matchesPredicate(interaction, predicate)` checks the attribute conditions and, if present, delegates content checks to the content matcher.\n\n**Implication.** Predicate evaluation is centralized, so all matching semantics (attribute equality, content match) live in one auditable spot.\n\n**Gotcha.** A predicate with no content clause is a pure attribute check — fast and content-free; only predicates that *opt in* to a `ContentPredicate` ever touch raw text."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/deviation/contentMatcher.ts",
      "line": 78,
      "title": "Matching content safely",
      "description": "**Situation.** Content predicates may use substrings or regexes supplied by the user in settings.\n\n**Mechanism.** `matchesContent(value, predicate)` performs the substring/regex test against the attribute's value.\n\n**Implication.** This enables precise rules (\"the PR title starts with a ticket id\") while staying entirely local.\n\n**Gotcha.** User-supplied regexes are dangerous — a crafted pattern can cause catastrophic backtracking (ReDoS). The next step is the guard that prevents it."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/deviation/contentMatcher.ts",
      "line": 123,
      "title": "ReDoS guard on user regexes",
      "description": "**Situation.** Because the regex source comes from settings, an accidental (or malicious) pattern could hang the extension host.\n\n**Mechanism.** `isSafeRegexSource(pattern)` screens the pattern for known catastrophic-backtracking constructs before it is ever compiled/executed.\n\n**Implication.** This is a concrete OWASP-style input-validation control at a trust boundary: untrusted pattern in → safety check → only then run.\n\n**Gotcha.** An unsafe pattern is refused rather than executed, so a bad workflow regex disables *that* content check instead of freezing the UI. Keep this guard in front of any new regex execution path."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/deviation/localDeviations.ts",
      "line": 31,
      "title": "Wiring detection into the panel",
      "description": "**Situation.** The session-detail webview (Tour 4) overlays deviation markers on turns — it needs a ready-to-call detector.\n\n**Mechanism.** `LocalDeviationDetector` composes the pieces: `detectForSession` (line 45) groups a session's interactions and runs `WorkflowDeviationDetector.detectForTurns` against the configured workflows, returning per-turn deviations.\n\n**Implication.** This is the object `activate()` handed to the `SessionDetailPanelManager`; the markers you see in the panel come from right here.\n\n**Gotcha.** It reads the workflow configs live from `Configuration`, so editing the workflow in settings changes markers on the next render — no reload required."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/notify/workflowDivergenceNotifier.ts",
      "line": 36,
      "title": "Proactive divergence toasts",
      "description": "**Situation.** Beyond the panel, teams want to be *told* when a run diverges, without staring at the view.\n\n**Mechanism.** `WorkflowDivergenceNotifier` (off by default) holds an `openSession` callback (line 44) and, on `refresh()` (line 48), scans recently-active sessions in repositories that have configured workflows.\n\n**Implication.** Divergences surface as actionable VS Code notifications; clicking one calls `openSession` to jump straight to the offending session's detail (line 157).\n\n**Gotcha.** These canonical line numbers differ from the `.claude/worktrees/` copy — always edit the `src/extension/...` file. The notifier is entirely local: it toasts, it doesn't transmit."
    },
    {
      "file": "src/extension/agent-observability-vscode/src/notify/workflowDivergenceNotifier.ts",
      "line": 63,
      "title": "Only NEW divergences fire",
      "description": "**Situation.** Re-scanning on every refresh would re-toast the same old divergence repeatedly — notification spam.\n\n**Mechanism.** `scan()` compares against a baseline of already-seen divergences and only notifies on newly-appeared ones; `resetBaseline()` (line 57) clears that memory when appropriate.\n\n**Implication.** The user is alerted once per genuinely new divergence, making the feature usable rather than noisy.\n\n**Gotcha.** The baseline is in-memory session state; a reload resets it, which is intentional — a fresh window re-establishes 'what's new' rather than persisting a notification ledger."
    },
    {
      "title": "Deviation recap",
      "description": "The whole feature is declarative and local: a **workflow schema** → **parsed** configs → interactions **grouped into turns** → matched by the **detector** (`detectForTurns` / `matchesPredicate`) with **safe content regexes** (guarded by `isSafeRegexSource`) → **markers** in the detail panel (`LocalDeviationDetector`) → and de-duplicated **toasts** (`WorkflowDivergenceNotifier`). Raw content is read only locally to evaluate predicates; nothing is uploaded.\n\nNext, cross the one boundary that *does* leave the machine — the privacy-safe aggregate. Continue to **Privacy-Safe Aggregation and Cloud Sync**."
    }
  ]
}
